# FileSwitch as filing systems see it, second round: a logging filing
# system registered from application space three times — TxLog (loads
# and saves through Open), TxRaw (through File 255 and File 0) and TxBit
# (an image filing system's host, canonicalising its discs through
# Func 23) — and an image filing system for files of type &ABC. Each
# records its entry calls and UpCall 3 for them: the reason, the names it
# is handed, counts, sizes and offsets, never an address, a handle or the
# time. A directory's listing is the root's subd, onef and twof, or any
# other's onef and twof, each read as it is listed; any other name is
# answered by its last character: `$`, `d` or `e` a directory (`e` the
# larger), `f` a file of 1000 bytes in 256-byte blocks, `l` the same
# locked, `x` the same with every attribute bit set but two, `u` an
# untyped file of 300 bytes, unbuffered, `i` an image of 2048 bytes, `n`
# a file only once made. A rename succeeds to a name ending in `n`; an
# object deleted is gone, from its directory's listing too, until a
# scenario forgets what was deleted.

scenario fileswitch-copying
block fs 12288
